d the  capital in a third  mark  protesters are literally storming the  capital  breaking windows on doors  rushing in  is trump going to say something  a fourth  there's an armed standoff at the house  chamber door  and another from someone inside the  capitol  we are all helpless  dozens of texts including from trump  administration officials  urged immediate action by the president  quote  potus has to come out firmly and tell  the protesters to dissipate  someone is going to get killed  in another mark he needs to stop this  now  a third in all caps  tell them to go  home  a fourth  and i quote  potus needs to calm this [ __ ] down  indeed  according to the records multiple fox  news hosts  knew the president needed to act  immediately  they texted mr meadows  and he has turned over those texts  quote  mark  the president needs to tell people in  the capitol to go home  this is hurting all of us he is  destroying his legacy  laura ingram wrote  please get him on tv  destroying everything you have  accomplished brian kilmeade texted  quote can he make a statement ask people  to leave the capitol  sean hannity urged  as the violence continued  one of the president's sons  texted mr meadows quote  he's got to condemn this [ __ ] asap  the capitol police tweet is not enough  donald trump jr texted  meadows responded  quote i'm pushing it hard i agree  still  president trump did not immediately act  donald trump jr texted  again  and again  urging action by the president  quote  we need an oval office address  he has to lead now  it has gone too far  and gotten out of hand end quote  but hours passed  without necessary action by the  president  these non-privileged texts are further  evidence  of president trump's supreme dereliction  of duty  during those 187 minutes  and mr meadows testimony will bear on  another key question before this  committee  did donald trump  through action or inaction  corruptly seek to obstruct or impede  congress's official proceedings to count  electoral votes  mark meadows testimony is necessary to  inform our legislative judgments okay so  that's a lot of different things that  she said  but at the end she says did donald trump  corruptly seek to obstruct or impede  congress's proceedings  this is being considered  the most direct signal thus far  that there is the  possibility  that donald trump might be charged with  pretty serious federal crimes what  cheney read there relates to 18 u.s code  subsection 1505 obstruction of  proceedings before departments  agencies and committees the issue here  of course  being donald trump's attempt to obstruct  investigation  into himself  and also  attempt to obstruct the proceedings  which would  correctly  assess joe biden the winner of the 2020  election and it reads whoever with  intent to avoid evade prevent or  obstruct compliance  with any civil investigative demand duly  improperly made  willfully withholds misrepresents  removes from any place conceals covers  up etc etc shall be fined under this  title imprisoned not more than five  years or if it involves international or  domestic terrorism up to eight years so  the idea here is that liz cheney is  hinting at possible criminal charges for  donald trump  over  the entire situation now that has  exploded for donald trump's former chief  of staff mark meadows involving text  messages involving a powerpoint which  very directly said here's how we would  do the coup and hear the different  elements that we would look at doing  there  a number of different legal analysts are  talking about this harvard law school  professor emeritus lawrence tribe  pointed out that refers specifically to  u.s code subsection 1505 18 us code  subsection 1505  and um cnn's ellie honig made a similar  comment when he was interviewed by john  berman and so this is now  going to be a question of whether or not  donald trump  could get away  with claiming presidential immunity that  would be the next layer here imagine  th